_irNotes: {
	"decision-absolute-recount": "Every derived count is an absolute recount over the source table, never a delta — and every mapping unions the triggering event's own key. All three pipelines are cdc, and the emitted shape (emit.cue:190-245) is a redis-streams input, a branch that GETs ${CRUD_URL}/<source>?<aggregate> with the service token, one bloblang, and a retrying POST to ${CRUD_URL}/<sink>?on_conflict=<pk> carrying Prefer: resolution=merge-duplicates. The CDC event is a trigger, never a payload: the emitted rows are a pure function of the source table's current contents, so replaying an event — or any other event, since every cdc pipeline reads the whole cdc-events stream and from is documentation rather than a filter — recomputes the same values and overwrites them identically. At-least-once delivery is therefore indistinguishable from exactly-once, and out-of-order delivery converges because no value depends on arrival order. Deltas were rejected for the reason the prelude states: at-least-once means a delta applied twice is simply wrong, and nothing in the transport can promise otherwise. The one subtlety, and it is load-bearing: an absolute read over the source cannot see a subject that has just lost its last row, so each mapping unions the event row's own key into the emitted set — without it, the last un-favorite leaves the count frozen at 1 forever, contradicting accept-favorite-toggles. That depends on conduit's restore-deleted-row processor back-filling Payload.After from Payload.Before on deletes, which is present in this tree (emit.cue:745-760). Each mapping also throws rather than degrade when the harness hands it a bare array at the root, because a groups-only count would silently leave stale finals behind. The second subtlety is the cost of that shared stream, and it is the reason each mapping's guard drops the message rather than reasserting the truth. Six tables are in the publication; resolution=merge-duplicates is ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE, which fires the emitted txid restamp trigger (emit.cue:677-696) on every row it touches; so a no-op reassertion would give every article_stats, favorite_index and tag_count row a fresh WAL version on every unrelated comment, and Electric would fan the whole sink out to every connected tab. The write and sync amplification is O(sink) per unrelated event, which is not harmlessness. root = deleted() on a foreign event costs one GET and no write, and the discriminator is exact per pipeline: user_id occurs only on favorite among the crud tables (article and comment carry author_id, follow carries follower_id), and tag only on article_tag (article carries tags). The maintenance trap is stated where it will be needed: a future crud table carrying one of those column names silently re-opens the storm. pipeline-designer fired here on its \"insert, update and delete must all be handled\" clause"
	"decision-article-tag-trigger": "ArticleTag is a trigger's output, not a pipeline's — the one place this design refuses the recount discipline, and why. The platform has no array type and one form writes one row, so Article.tags is a single text column: the words as the writer typed them, round-tripped by the editor. Three things need tag rows: the /tag/:name inner-embed list, the chips on every preview (a nested region cannot split a string), and tag-recount, which needs a crud-path source because the publication covers crud tables only. A pipeline cannot produce them: an absolute recount can re-assert the pairs an article has, but the CDC event carries the after-image only, so it can never retract the pair it no longer has — retagging would leave stale rows and a dishonest popular-tags panel, contradicting accept-tagcount-true. 013_article_tag_sync.sql is delete-and-reinsert inside the article's own transaction, which is exact by construction and makes the piece appear on each tag's list the moment it appears on the community list (accept-tags-file). SECURITY DEFINER because article_tag carries no app_user write policy at all; deletion needs no arm because the FK cascades. The IS NOT DISTINCT FROM guard is not decoration: the editor's update form always names tags (AFTER UPDATE OF fires on the column being named, not changed), so without it every title fix would churn the whole tag set, replace every chip's DOM node and wake the pipeline for nothing. The corollary the form contract must keep: the revise form always submits all four fields, because omitting tags would silently freeze the projection"
	"decision-auth-deviation": "Conduit takes the terminal's door and therefore has no anonymous reader — the one place this app deliberately departs from the RealWorld specification. RealWorld specifies email/password registration with a user-chosen username and a JWT the app manages. The terminal publishes its offer as data — capabilities.auth.modes: [\"passkey\", \"guest\"], identity: \"generated\" (plugins/omnishell/terminal.cue:56-65) — and #emit refuses any other mode, so an email/password program does not compile. The gate is also binary: shell.js:254-266 checks auth.required before a store is created or a screen is mounted, renders its own login chrome when no session exists, and with required: false issues no Authorization header at all — under which anon holds schema USAGE and no table privileges (emit.cue:603,614), so an ungated Conduit would read nothing. There is no anonymous-read-with-optional-sign-in mode to ask for. Two designs were therefore rejected outright: a hand-written sign-in screen with an email field, and a public browse mode with a sign-in prompt on write. Per the prelude's capability doctrine, demanding what a runtime does not offer is a compile error; the brief already names this in Out of scope (\"the door is the platform's and it comes first\") and the identity section spells the ceremony out. Accepted losses, recorded rather than smoothed over: a search-engine crawler sees the login chrome and nothing else, a shared article link costs one guest click before it reads, and a guest identity lives in sessionStorage — closing the tab ends it, and only a passkey brings a person back to their own articles (accept-signout-return). What the app gets in exchange is the whole ceremony for free: one tap, no typing, a unique generated handle, the name · handle · sign out chrome, the nav, and 401 recovery, none of it designed here"
	"decision-author-card": "The writer's card sits after the body, and repeats the follow control there. The byline at the top sells the piece; the card at the foot sells the person, and that ask only makes sense to a reader who has just finished reading — which is the whole reason the card exists and the reason the ask belongs in it. Its identity costs no region: every value comes from the author embed the article row already carries, which is why bio joined that select rather than arriving as a second read — the byline and the card read the same row and so can never disagree about a name. Reversed from “no follow button here”: the objection was that two controls for one state are two places to be wrong about it, and that is only true of two controls with two sources of truth. These have one. The card's arms are the same two declared forms with the same filters, chosen by a probe with the same followed_id=eq.{author_id} — the same shape, so the two controls settle together on the same refresh and a reader cannot see “Follow” in one and “Following” in the other. What the top of the piece cannot do is catch the reader at the moment they have decided, because by then it is a screen and a half above them"
	"decision-bookmark-no-public-face": "A save is a favorite with its derived half deleted, and that deletion is the feature. Bookmark is owned on user_id exactly as Favorite is, but where a favorite has two public faces — ArticleStats for the count everyone sees, FavoriteIndex for the list somebody else can read — a save has none. Nobody may see that you saved a piece, no number moves when you do, and so there is no sink, no pipeline, and none of the seconds of lag the derived faces cost. The reading list is therefore read straight off the owned table, which is what lets it be ordered by save time, the thing a reader means by “my list”; profile-favorites cannot do that, because its order has to come from a sink. Two alternatives were rejected. Folding saves into Favorite: one gesture cannot mean both “this was good” and “not yet”, and a reader who hearts a piece to find it again has corrupted the only public signal the app has. A public save count: it is the obvious next feature and it would change what people save — a reading list is a private intention, and an intention with an audience becomes a performance. ACCEPTED COST, and the measurement changed what it says. The star's probe is the seventh Electric shape on a list screen, and home already opened six (article, app_user, article_tag, article_stats, favorite, tag_count — counted live, one shape per table however many cards draw it). Six is Chrome's per-origin connection ceiling on HTTP/1.1, so the pool is fully consumed by long-polls and a write waits for one of them to return. This was written down as a mild cost and then measured, and it is not mild — from PerformanceResourceTiming on the running cluster: screenlive shapeswritestalledserver settings2PATCH app_user, 183 ms1 ms181 ms tag7POST bookmark, 12 151 ms12 073 ms78 ms Seventy-eight milliseconds of server against twelve seconds of stalled: the write is not slow, it is queued behind six long-polls for a connection. Two things follow, and only one of them is about this feature. The first: this is not a regression the save arm introduced — at six shapes the pool was already exhausted, so favoriting from home stalled exactly as long before the seventh shape existed (measured at 7 855 ms on the same page), and article has been over the ceiling at eight since it was written. The optimistic arms are what have hidden it: the star and the pill fill on the press, so the reader sees the write land and the twelve seconds are spent invisibly. The second, which is the one that matters: this is now the app's dominant defect, it is transport and not schema, and it has a known answer — the cluster's own :8443 listener is TLS and therefore h2, which has no per-origin cap, while the human door on :8080 is plain HTTP/1.1 because a browser will not negotiate h2 in clear. Gating a feature every reading surface has on that artifact would be optimising the wrong thing; leaving the number unwritten would be worse. The rule it leaves for the next feature: on this app a new region is free and a new table is not"
	"decision-browser-tier-unsupported": "The two pipelines still written as bloblang declare the browser tier unsupported rather than quietly broken. A cdc pipeline that is not a fold must ship a browser shim (the file must exist), and that shim's contract is (rows) => singleRowObject upserted on id — but favorite-index and tag-recount both emit a keyed array of sink rows, one per subject, which that contract cannot express. Each is therefore a stub that throws naming this decision, rather than a plausible-looking function: an unsupported tier that fails loudly is a known gap; one that returns a number is a lie. favorite-recount is no longer among them: it declares a browser fold (decision 37), which states a keyed aggregate the shim contract cannot. The remaining two are the same conversion, unstarted"
	"decision-comment-plain-text": "Comment bodies are plain text; only article bodies are markdown. The same binding is one attribute away, so this is a choice and not a limit. The brief describes a comment as \"its text, its writer, and when it was left\" and describes an article body as markdown, in the same section — the asymmetry is the author's. A markdown reply is a formatting surface by another route, and the brief puts formatting surfaces (a toolbar, a live preview) out of scope; a one-line reply that renders # as a heading is a paper cut, not a feature. Comments still read as authored text under DESIGN.md's duality — serif, --primary, at the same measure — so the identity holds without the renderer. Reversible in one attribute if the ux seat argues otherwise"
	"decision-composite-uniques": "Three composite uniques live in raw SQL, and their violations are the app's duplicate refusals. #Field.unique is per-field, so (user_id, article_id) on favorite, (follower_id, followed_id) on follow and (article_id, tag) on article_tag are 010_composite_uniques.sql. The refusal path matters and is stated once here: the Caddyfile injects Prefer: return=representation,resolution=ignore-duplicates on POSTs, and PostgREST's ignore-duplicates targets the primary key, which a client-minted uuid never collides on — so a repeated favorite reaches uq_favorite_pair and comes back 23505 as a readable .store-error (\"You've already favorited this one.\"), not a silent merge. With the pill reading its true state from the probe that only happens on a double-press inside the optimistic window, and a refusal there is honest. This is what makes accept-favorite-once true at the database rather than at the button. Recorded alternative, not taken: ?on_conflict=user_id,article_id on the write would merge silently, but forms cannot express it; dropping the index would surrender the acceptance id outright"
	"decision-cover-credit": "A cover carries a credit line, and it is one nullable column rather than a caption system. The picture on a piece is almost always somebody else's, and showing it without saying whose is a claim the app has not earned — every surface this one is measured against runs a credit under the image. What it is not is a caption: no rich text, no link, no second alt story, 120 characters. That ceiling is the decision. A caption field invites a second body — writers put sentences in it, then markdown, then links — and the piece already has a body and a standfirst for that. It costs no region. The caption lives inside the cover's own one-row region, which exists already because a bare <img> bound to an unset column would hold its box open on every piece without a picture; so a credit renders exactly where a cover does and nowhere else, and :empty collapses it where the writer supplied none — the same rule that collapses an unset display name in a byline, against the same problem. Nullable rather than required for the same reason: a writer who took the photograph has nobody to credit, and a required field would put “own work” on half the pieces in the app"
	"decision-cover-link": "A cover is a link the writer supplies, not an upload. capabilities.blobs is off, so there is no object store to put a file in and no resizer to put in front of it; a URL column costs a text field and reuses the treatment AppUser.image_url already has. Both the hero and the preview thumbnail are regions filtered on the column rather than bare <img> tags, because an unset URL binds to the terminal's transparent pixel — right for an avatar, whose box is drawn either way, and wrong for a hero, which would hold a 2:1 box open on every piece that has no cover. The cost is the cost of any link: it can rot, and nothing here can notice. That is accepted for the same reason the profile picture accepts it"
	"decision-design-identity": "Press, over Conduit-classic and Terminal. The designer charter requires at least three directions with recorded verdicts before an identity commits, and DESIGN.md carries the comparison. Press — warm paper ground, near-black ink, one deep pine accent spent only where something is created or committed, and a serif/sans duality in which everything a person wrote is serif and everything the app says about it is sans — was chosen because the duality does the work a second colour would otherwise have to do, which lets the palette stay quiet across a long scroll of previews; because the article body's reading measure is native to the identity rather than a special case on its most important screen; and because the dark twin keeps its warmth by lowering value rather than shifting hue, so it is a token swap and never a redesign. Conduit-classic — the RealWorld reference look, white ground, bright #5CB85C, all-sans — was rejected as the identity this exercise exists to beat: the green competes with every preview title, and an all-sans setting makes a body read like a form field. Terminal — near-black in both appearances, monospace metadata — was rejected for flattering short chrome and punishing long prose, and because a dark-only ground makes the light appearance a retrofit when the brief asks for both to read correctly (accept-dual-appearance). The contract carried forward: the token names are the schema contract (colors, dark, rounded, spacing travel verbatim into the program), so the values may evolve without a migration — and a screen stylesheet may consume them with var() but may never redeclare one, which check-screens enforces as an error. Three bindings this entry has to make explicit, because DESIGN.md legislates them and an omission is an answer. The chrome. #Design.shell (schema.cue:31-35) defaults to {bg: \"neutral\", fg: \"primary\", rule: \"border\"}, which emit.cue:526-528 resolves to --shell-fg: var(--primary) and shell.css paints onto body with nav a { color: inherit } — so the default would render the nav row and “velvet-crow-4 · sign out” in ink. This app declares shell: {bg: \"neutral\", fg: \"secondary\", rule: \"border\"}: the nav is chrome and reads at --secondary, which is what every frame draws, and the consequence is named rather than discovered — this also becomes body's default colour, which every screen overrides for authored text anyway, since authored text is exactly what the type roles already promote to --primary. The wordmark. DESIGN.md's one absolute Don't is “chrome in serif”, and home's masthead sets Conduit in serif at display size. That is the single exemption this identity grants, and it is granted here rather than left to be argued: a masthead is a publication's name-plate, not navigation — it is the one place the app speaks in the voice of the writing rather than about it, it appears on one route, and it is never a link to anywhere. The navigation gloss for / is therefore Community in sans, so the word Conduit appears once per screen and always as the name-plate. Type. The five DESIGN.md roles are bound to surfaces in the Screens intro's role table, and test-dual-appearance carries the matching assertion beside its no-literal-hex clause: a screen stylesheet may set family and size only through the role classes, never inline. Colour had a checkable rule and type had none, which left the duality that is this identity resting on prose and on frames measured by eye"
	"decision-earlier-strip": "The strip under a piece lists that writer's earlier work, and the cursor is what excludes the piece being read. The local filter vocabulary has eq and the four cursor comparisons and no neq, so \"everything else by this writer\" cannot be expressed — created_at=lt.{created_at} can, and it drops the current piece by construction rather than by a rule someone has to maintain. It also reads better than it computes: earlier is a claim the ordering already supports. It reads the Article table the screen has synced anyway, so the strip costs a region and no new shape — which is the budget that decides what else this screen may carry"
	"decision-edit-route-by-id": "The read route is keyed on the slug, the edit route on the id. The slug is GENERATED ALWAYS … STORED over the title (decision 9), so saving a retitled piece changes its address in the same transaction that saves it. A screen filtered slug=eq.{param.slug} would find zero rows the instant the save landed and blank itself under the writer's hands — an editor that destroys its own context on success. Keyed on id=eq.{param.id} the row survives the retitle, and the success state can offer Read it → linking /article/{slug} with the new address. The reader never types this route: it is reached only from an owner's Edit control, and a hand-typed one for somebody else's piece is refused loudly (decision 5). Segment counts keep /editor (2) and /editor/:id (3) unambiguous, which the router requires since it has no optional segments"
	"decision-escape-hatches": "Escape hatches: none. Argued, not assumed. The ranked ladder is pre-compiled WASM behind a CUE contract, then an external API endpoint, then a container with a declared browser-tier shim; this app takes no rung of it. Everything Conduit does is schema, access policy, trigger, pipeline or screen markup: ten entities and their CEL constraints, five raw migrations, three cdc pipelines, twelve screens, and zero handlers — the only event source wired to a handler is drag and nothing here reorders. The one Jessie module this app ships is the article screen's markdown renderer, and a renderer is a declared seam rather than a hatch: it runs in a compartment with nothing endowed, and what it may produce is the terminal's tag and attribute allowlist rather than the module's own choice. The five raw migrations are the one thing that looks like an escape and is not: rawMigrations is a declared seam in schema.cue, its files are checked-in SQL a reviewer reads, they are emitted in the same numbered sequence as the derived DDL, and the two triggers among them exist because the platform's own alternative would be wrong rather than merely absent (decisions 3 and 10 each say why). The one place an escape was genuinely on the table was markdown rendering, and the answer was the terminal's renderer role (decision 16) — which is the doctrine working: the platform owns what a rendering may safely become, and the app owns only which markdown subset it wants. Consequently the emitted bayt.cue redeclares out.cluster unchanged and the program's cluster: unification carries nothing — the two places a hatch would be visible are both empty, which is what \"none\" has to mean to be checkable"
	"decision-favorite-private-derived": "Favorite is private; its two public faces are derived tables. The pill must answer \"have I favorited this\" on every card of every list, on a fresh visit, without a round trip — so Favorite is owned on user_id and the probe read favorite?article_id=eq.{id} is in the locally translatable subset, resolving in-tab against the synced collection and flipping optimistically on the press. That privacy costs two public facts, and each is bought back by a pipeline rather than by weakening the mode: the count everyone sees is ArticleStats via favorite-recount, and another person's favorited list — required by accept-profile-page — is FavoriteIndex via favorite-index, whose active boolean is how an upsert-only sink retracts an un-favorite. The pipeline-designer card fired on both, and two of its three rules hold: the value is read on every render of a list screen (hot path), and more than one surface consumes it. Rejected: making Favorite public-read and answering the toggle with a per-card embed through me — correct, but one server round trip per card re-issued on every dependency change; and a trigger-maintained public mirror — exact and instant, but a third trigger where the platform already publishes an answer for cross-user views. Two accepted losses, both named rather than left to be measured. The list lags a favorite by pipeline latency (seconds); no acceptance id demands otherwise, and the reader's own pill on those cards is optimistic regardless. And the card itself costs a round trip: each favorited row nests a live Article read whose aliased embed parseSelect rejects (data-crud.js:109-114), so the region is server-computed — up to twenty GETs per paint, re-fired whenever article or app_user changes, and app_user changes whenever anybody saves their settings. It is bounded by the list's own limit=20 and it is the price of the preview being the live row: denormalising title, description and byline into FavoriteIndex would remove the fetch and break accept-edit-propagates and accept-profile-propagates for this one list, which is a worse trade than a bounded fetch on the app's least-travelled screen. decision 13 is the other half of this: without a foreign key on article_id there is no single embedded-filter read that could replace the twenty"
	"decision-feed-read": "Your Feed is one read, and it names no reader — RLS supplies the identity the binding grammar cannot. The read is article?select=*,author:app_user!inner(handle,display_name,image_url,follow!inner(follower_id))&order=created_at.desc&limit=20. Two measurements make it work (probed live against a running mecha cluster on 2026-08-02): an !inner embed drops parents whose child set is empty, and the inner-ness propagates up through a second level (M1); and RLS decides what an embedded resource can see, so follow!inner sees only rows where follower_id = auth_uid() (M2). Together: every article whose author I do not follow has an empty follow embed, fails the inner join, and is dropped — with no filter naming me, because nothing can name me. embedTables (data-crud.js:100-101) puts both app_user and follow in the region's dependency set, so a new follow fills the feed without a navigation (accept-follow-fills-feed). Rejected: a feed sink materialised by a pipeline (a whole entity and pipeline, seconds of lag on a follow, and the brief says \"immediately\"); a denormalised follower_ids text column on Article (no array type, and a second definition of who follows whom); and client-side filtering (there is no client-side computation). The belt-and-braces fallback, if the inner-ness ever fails to propagate against Conduit's own constraint graph, is an explicit embedded filter &author.follow.follower_id=not.is.null — recorded here because it must be a decision, not a debugging discovery. QA charter: this read is the first thing hop 3 verifies against the running cluster with an app_user token"
	"decision-feed-route": "The two reading modes are two routes, not two lists on one screen. Only top-level regions drive the state machine and there is no arbitration between siblings: each sets empty or populated from its own rows (screen.js:843-845), so the last to refresh wins. On a combined home the loser would be the feed's empty state — precisely the one the brief singles out (\"tells a reader with no follows yet how to fill it, rather than showing them nothing\") and precisely the one accept-feed-follows-only checks. As routes they also earn nav chrome, a back stack entry and a shareable address for free, and the nav row the terminal builds reads Community · Your Feed · New Article · Settings — the home route glossed Community so the word Conduit belongs to the masthead alone (decision 26) — which is the whole navigation this app needs. Rejected: a Zag tabs widget (widgets feed form values, not region visibility — widget.js, screen.js:696-712), and CSS :target (the hash belongs to the router). The general rule this compile follows: a screen with more than one top-level region styles empty and populated alike and lets each region's own data-empty sentence speak; a screen with one styles empty as the whole screen"
	"decision-follow-single-fk": "Follow carries foreign keys on both of its columns, and the feed read names its relationship — the platform was extended rather than the schema contorted. Two foreign keys from follow to app_user makes the embed ambiguous and PostgREST answers 300 PGRST201 naming every candidate relationship (measured directly: two throwaway tables with two foreign keys to app_user were created in a dev database, probed, and dropped). The documented cure is a hint — follow!followed_id!inner(…) — and at the time the data design was written that hint broke the interpreter: embedTables matched at most one !hint and captured followed_id as the table name, dropping follow from the feed region's dependency set and making the feed deaf to a new follow (M4). An earlier revision of this design bought its way out by dropping the second foreign key, which removed the ambiguity at its root and kept the read unhinted — at the cost of referential integrity and ON DELETE CASCADE on the column that points at a person. That interpreter defect is fixed: the character class was made repeating, (?:![a-z_][a-z0-9_]*)* (plugins/omnishell/interpreter/data-crud.js:101), with a regression assertion in interpreter/pending-smoke.js citing accept-follow-fills-feed. The fix was measured directly on the fragment this app needs — \"*,author:app_user!inner(handle,follow!followed_id!inner(follower_id))\" went from [\"app_user\", \"followed_id\"] (deaf) to [\"app_user\", \"follow\"]. So this revision takes the shape the prelude prefers: both columns carry REFERENCES app_user(id) ON DELETE CASCADE, and feed and feed-older read *,author:app_user!inner(handle,display_name,image_url,follow!followed_id!inner(follower_id)). The hint is the FK column name rather than the constraint name, because the column name does not depend on what Postgres decided to call the constraint. Extending the terminal beat contorting the schema, which is the doctrine working rather than a principle admired; the schema keeps its integrity, an account-deletion feature would cascade correctly if it ever ships, and the one residual risk is that a PGRST201 renders as an empty region rather than an error — which is why this read is the first thing hop 3 runs live with an app_user token (decision 7)"
	"decision-identity-chrome": "The reader's own identity in the strip is a link, not a label. capabilities.auth.self hands the terminal the route that is a person's own page (/profile/:handle, its param filled from the session user) and the column the name they chose lives in (app_user.display_name), and the strip draws name · handle · sign out with the name linking there (shell.js:198-241). Three things were decided together and are one decision because each is only right given the others. The name is read live, not stamped from the token: the JWT carries handle and the auth service is app-agnostic by doctrine — it inserts (id, handle) and knows nothing of a display name — so a token-stamped strip would keep reading the old name until the next sign-in, on the one surface a person sees on every screen, while every byline had already changed. The strip follows the same name-then-handle rule as a byline, .name:empty collapsing for anyone who has set none (decision 23's rule, applied to chrome), because a person named one way in the content and another way in the chrome is the inconsistency the rule exists to prevent. And settings comes off the strip (strip: false, the one opt-out the nav has): one's own page now leads there, a second top-level path to a screen visited once crowded the one identity that is on screen always, and the strip did not fit at a phone width with both. What hangs on it is not cosmetic: a person's own writing lives on profile, and the only other door to one's own is the “last published” list on editor — so without this link the one piece of identity a reader has on every screen is the one that leads nowhere"
	"decision-markdown-capability": "Article bodies render as markdown through this app's own renderer module, and the terminal owns the half of that job which carries the safety property. The brief asks for a body \"in markdown — headings, emphasis, links, lists, quotes and code, written plainly and read as formatting\", and the terminal's built-in formats are plain and datetime alone: the honest options were a WASM hatch around a CommonMark parser, a Jessie handler (which cannot run — handlers fire only on drag), or shipping plain text and failing accept-body-markdown. The terminal's answer is the renderer role — data-text-format names a built-in format or an app module resolved by basename out of the route's files.renderers, exactly as data-handler resolves (screen.js:106-130) — so the format vocabulary grows per app rather than per platform release. This app declares shell/renderers/markdown.js on the article screen alone, the one screen that renders a body. A parser is not a platform affordance the way datetime is: there is no single right markdown subset, and an app that wants tables or maths should not be waiting on a terminal release to get them. The body binds as <div class=\"body\" data-text=\"{body}\" data-text-format=\"markdown\"></div> and the screen styles the resulting elements at DESIGN.md's 65ch measure. The parser is app code; what it is allowed to produce is not. The parser returns a node description and the builder in render.js materialises it with createElement and textContent, never innerHTML — so there is no sanitizer in this design, and none is needed: a body containing <script> renders those literal characters, and a link whose scheme is not in the allowlist (http, https, mailto, or schemeless) renders as the author's own text rather than as a dead or dangerous link (render.js:23-38, accept-body-safe). A sanitizer is a denylist that must stay ahead of attackers; a builder that cannot express markup is a whitelist that cannot fall behind. The subset is deliberately smaller than CommonMark — paragraphs, ATX headings, unordered and ordered lists, blockquotes, fenced code, rules, hard breaks, and inline bold, italic, code, links and images; no tables, no nested lists, no reference links, no raw HTML — and the brief's Out of scope names the same four omissions, so the writer's vocabulary and the renderer's agree exactly rather than by luck. Rejected explicitly: a vendored markdown library at the terminal tier (capabilities.vendored exists but is outside the bijection, so nothing would check it), and a WASM hatch (a whole escape rung spent on a parser the platform should own — and it would have made decision 27 read \"one\", for the worse reason)"
	"decision-me-row": "Me is a table the brief never names, and it is the most load-bearing thing in the design. The interpreter has no session binding: lookup (screen.js:157) resolves {param.x} and dot paths into the current row and nothing else — there is no {user.id}, and a screen therefore cannot ask \"is this mine?\". Conduit asks four times: edit and delete on your own article (accept-author-only-controls), the × on your own comment (accept-comment-remove), \"Edit your profile\" instead of Follow on your own page (accept-no-self-follow), and a /settings route with no :param to identify you with. Me answers all four the only way the platform allows: one row per person, owned on id, so RLS has already decided what the reader may see and the row's mere visibility, joined against an author_id or a handle, is the answer. All four reads are col=eq.<v> with no select, so they run in-tab against the synced collection — no round trip per card. Written by a trigger (014_me_provision.sql) rather than a pipeline because the row must exist on the first paint after a one-click guest sign-in and a CDC round trip is seconds; SECURITY DEFINER because the auth service inserts app_user under the service role. 012_me_readonly.sql then drops the INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE arms owned emits, which is narrowing and therefore always safe: nothing should write this table from a browser, and a person deleting their own row would lock themselves out of their own settings screen. Rejected: duplicating the reader's uuid into every table as a denormalised is_mine-style column (a lie the moment two people read the same row), and a per-card server read joining app_user to itself (correct, one round trip per card, re-fetched on every dependency change)"
	"decision-narrow-recount": "A recount emits the group its event touched, and no others. The count stays absolute — it is the length of the fetched set, never a delta, which is what decision 9 asks for — but the scope of the emission is one article. Re-emitting every group was the earlier shape, and it was expensive in a way that reached the screen: merge-duplicates is ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE, the restamp_txid trigger fires on any UPDATE including one writing an unchanged count, so a favorite anywhere restamped every article_stats row. This mapping already refuses that for foreign messages, for exactly this reason; it simply had not applied the rule within its own table's events. Two things follow. The write amplification goes: one row moves per event, not the whole sink. And article_stats.txid starts meaning “this count was last recomputed”, where before it meant “something, somewhere, was recounted” — which is the difference between a gate an optimistic counter can trust and one that flickers (decision 38). Measured: a favorite on one piece now moves that row's txid and leaves the other five untouched. ACCEPTED LOSS. Recounting every article made a dropped event self-healing — the next event for any article repaired all of them. Narrowed, a dropped event leaves that one article's count stale until something touches it again. The absolute read survives; the incidental repair does not, and no test covered it, which is its own argument for writing it down here"
	"decision-one-row-list": "Any region that must contain another region is a one-row list region, never a singleton. hydrateRegion's singleton branch binds attributes, texts and hatches and never calls syncNested; only the list branch does (screen.js:726,785 vs the singleton branch at 847-880), and the top-level scan skips any region whose ancestor is a region. A [data-live] nested under a singleton is therefore hydrated by nobody — dead markup that lints clean and renders nothing, which is the worst failure shape available. So /article/:slug, /settings's me root, /editor's me root, /editor/:id, both profile headers and each favorited row's article preview are all lists that happen to match one row: they bind identically, they recurse for their children, and their forms get a real row id. One consequence is named rather than absorbed: a list region with zero rows sets empty, never gone (screen.js:843-845), so the article screen does not declare gone and its vanished-piece frame is empty carrying the gone copy (\"That piece is gone.\"). Nothing is lost — the two states differ only in name here — and the alternative, a singleton root, would cost that screen every nested region it has: tags, comments, stats, and three probes"
	"decision-optimistic-count": "The favorite count IS projected optimistically, and what made that hard was that the reader is inside the aggregate and cannot see the rest of it. The arm was always optimistic — the row lands locally under 100 ms — while the number beside it is ArticleStats, everyone's, a CDC loop behind. Favorite is private, so a browser holds its own rows and no one else's; the count holds everybody. The reader is a term in a sum whose other terms they may not read. What the terminal shows is others + intent. Intent is local fact and applies at once, in either direction. others needs the reader's own place in the public total, and that is a property of the READ that produced the total rather than of the data now — so it is stated by whoever performed the read, not reconstructed by whoever consumes it. favorite-count emits it per reader into FavoriteCount, which RLS keeps private because a count discloses nobody. An earlier attempt put the same arithmetic in the markup and failed on a read skew — a relational comparison is not maintainable, so the gate asked PostgREST while the value came from the Electric-synced collection, and the number dipped between them (measured 7, 8, 7, 8, the dip at 178 ms). Four later attempts tried to infer the reader's contribution from their own row and each failed at a different number of changes, because a bounded row cannot carry unbounded history. The full account, with what each attempt cost and the platform facts it rests on, is plugins/pronto/docs/2026-08-12-derived-counts-the-reader-is-inside.md. Two findings survive from that search regardless: a singleton region wrapping a templated child adopts that child's template and clears everything else in it, and decision 37, which was discovered while chasing this and is worth keeping on its own"
	"decision-optimistic-fold": "The sink carries a read watermark, and the browser resumes the count over the rows that watermark excludes. A sink lags its source by the whole CDC loop — near a second — so favouriting a piece changed its heart instantly while the number beside it sat still. The browser transform is declared as a fold: empty(key), step(acc, row), combine(a, b), result(acc). Two laws no type states. combine is associative with empty as its identity, so a fold splits and total = fold(counted) ⊕ fold(not yet counted). And the accumulator IS the sink row, so the sink reads back as a partial fold — which is why an average would store sum and n rather than the quotient. The fold also replaces the browser shim, whose (rows) => singleRow contract could not state a keyed aggregate at all. The watermark is a separate column and must be. article_stats.counted_txid is the newest favorite.txid the count includes, emitted by the transform from the rows it actually read. The sink's own txid cannot serve: it is stamped when the sink row is written, strictly after the read it summarises, so every favourite committed in that window looks older than the sink while never having been counted. Reading it that way silently drops the reader's own favourite and settles on a count one too low beside a filled heart. Clicking slowly never opens the window; a reader clicking quickly lives inside it. Contributions collapse on the natural key. Toggling fast leaves several pending rows for one (user_id, article_id) pair in the optimistic overlay, and the unique constraint means the server can hold exactly one — summing them showed totals no state of the database could produce. A row the sink has already counted also outranks a pending duplicate of itself, which is what stops the two being added together when the sink's update overtakes the row's own confirmation. Both sides of the comparison read the local collections and never the server. An earlier attempt put the same arithmetic in the markup, where relational operators are not maintainable, so the gate asked PostgREST while the value came from Electric and the count dipped between two readers of one table. The container keeps its bloblang. rpk embeds goja and can run this module — a revision of this work did exactly that, on the argument that one implementation needs no equivalence check — but redpanda-connect lint rejects a broken mapping and accepts any JavaScript string whatsoever, so the sharing cost more in build-time validation than it bought. For a plain count the two sides are three one-line functions apart. Convert a container transform only where the aggregate is substantial enough that two expressions of it could diverge. The fold declares an inverse, and a retraction is a row rather than an absence. unstep(acc, row) undoes step, which makes this fold a group rather than only a monoid. Not every aggregate has one — count and sum do, max and min do not, since removing the extreme leaves no way to recover the next from the accumulator alone — so it is optional, and a fold without it waits for the loop. Un-favouriting SETS Favorite.deleted_at instead of deleting the row, and the pair stays unique, so there is exactly one row per reader per piece forever: the table cannot grow by toggling and needs no reaper. The point is not storage, it is that a retraction the reader can still SEE needs no memory of what was removed. An earlier revision kept removed rows in a per-tab map with a rule for when to retire them; that map, its retirement rule and its retraction bound are all gone, and the retraction now survives a reload and is visible to the reader's other tabs. The count reads only active rows; the WATERMARK reads all of them, retracted included. Filtering the transform's read to active rows would leave a just-retracted row above the watermark, where the browser reads it as a change the sink has not seen and subtracts it a second time. The two directions are one rule. A row places itself relative to the sink by txid — the last version the server acknowledged — and whether anything of the reader's sits on top of it here. Zero unseen changes means the sink is current; one means the row has moved once, and is worth step or unstep according to where it now points; two means it has moved and moved back, and is worth nothing, because assertion and retraction alternate. That parity is what stops re-favouriting inside one loop counting twice on a sink still holding the first assertion. Three or more unseen changes cannot be told from one, because the row keeps only its latest txid. That is the bound this design accepts in exchange for holding no per-tab memory, and it costs at most one for at most one loop"
	"decision-paging-keyset": "Long lists page by a keyset cursor the row already carries; four lists cap at twenty and say so, for cost rather than for a limit. limit and offset ride the filter string and work (measured against the running cluster), but there is no arithmetic anywhere in the binding grammar, so a screen cannot compute offset + 20. The way out is to page on a value that already exists: the twentieth card's own created_at, interpolated into an ordinary link — <a data-route=\"older\" data-param-when=\"{created_at}\">Older writing →</a>, revealed by .list > *:nth-child(20) .older { display: block } — and /older/:when filters created_at=lt.{param.when}&limit=20 and carries the same link again. There is no last page, no page count, and no number a screen had to invent. Two alternatives were live and both were rejected. A literal page-number ladder (/page/1…/page/5) is a fixed ceiling with dead links the day the sixth page exists. And — the interesting one — a pipeline-materialised page index: a pipeline can do arithmetic where a screen cannot, so a sink table of one row per page carrying its literal offset is genuinely expressible, and a pipeline is where the arithmetic a screen cannot do is allowed to happen. It was rejected here for cost and honesty: a whole entity and a fourth pipeline bought for one link, recomputed on every article write, and lagging the list it indexes by CDC latency — so the page ladder would disagree with the list it sits under for seconds after every publication. Keyset paging is exact, instant, and free. Three details of the reveal and the order are decisions rather than styling. The rule is :nth-child(20) and not :last-child: a three-article community would otherwise end its list with “Older writing →” one tap after being told to write the first piece, and land on “You've reached the beginning.” — a dead end offered as a way forward. Every paged list orders created_at.desc,id.desc, so the sequence is deterministic across the boundary; the residual loss is an accepted one and belongs here rather than deferred inside a test: two rows sharing a created_at to the microsecond straddle a strict lt. cursor and the second is dropped, because one :param cannot carry a composite cursor and there is no arithmetic to build one. It is reachable — two pieces inserted in one transaction, or seed data — and it is the price of paging without a page number. And feed pages to feed-older, never to older: the cheap version of this design pointed both at the community list, which silently dropped the follow filter under a heading the reader had no reason to distrust. The four capped lists, and why they are capped. tag, search, profile and profile-favorites show the newest twenty under a ceiling line that rides the item template (.list > *:nth-child(20) .ceiling { display: block }, so it appears only when a twentieth row exists) — “Showing the 20 newest. Narrow with a search or another tag.” on the two list screens, and “Showing the 20 newest. Search to find an older piece.” on the two profile screens, where “another tag” would mean nothing. This is a cost decision and not a router limit. An earlier revision of this entry claimed the router “matches an exact segment count with no optional segments … and their one :param is already spent”, which is false: matchRoute loops over every segment and collects every :name it meets (shell.js:229-234), nothing limits a route to one param, and this ir's own roster refutes it — /profile/:handle/favorites is a four-segment route beside a three-segment one, and feed-older is a fifth screen added on exactly that mechanism. /tag/:name/:when, /search/:q/:when and /profile/:handle/:when are all expressible today and would reuse older's link verbatim. They are declined because each costs a whole screen section, a storyboard, its own keep-budget and its own frames, bought for the tail of a list a reader rarely walks: a tag, a query and a person's own output are all naturally narrow, and the screen already offers the better instrument for reaching an older piece, which is the search box. Declining a capability the app plausibly wants is a Decisions entry by the prelude's own rule; this is that entry, argued on cost, and the false constraint is struck so the next compile does not inherit it. accept-list-paged is realized on home, older, feed and feed-older"
	"decision-preview-one-component": "Every preview is one component on eight screens, and the favorite count rides inside the button. DESIGN.md makes it a hard constraint — \"keep every preview identical wherever it appears… so the list is one component and reads as one\" — and accept-preview-complete names its six parts: byline avatar and handle, date, title, description, tags, favorite count. Home, feed, feed-older, older, tag, search, profile and profile-favorites all render the same markup — literally the same: the preview is one fragment file included eight times, which is a loop rule rather than a convention, because the claim is only checkable if the markup is shared. Its numbers are fixed here as numbers rather than adjectives, so a reviewer of the emitted CSS has something to check and a frame has something to be drawn at: a 212×62 well at rounded.md in surface with a border hairline; the avatar a r6.5 circle at rounded.full; the byline meta-sm in secondary, underlined, wrapping avatar, name and handle in one /profile/{author.handle} link; the date right-aligned meta-sm; the title display-md — serif, weight 600, DESIGN.md's full stack — the description prose serif at --primary; the chips surface-muted at half their own height's radius; the pill 42×13 at rounded.full. Insets are token multiples: spacing.sm (8px) inside the well, spacing.sm between cards, which at the storyboard's 0.615× scale is the 8 and 6 frame units the frames are drawn at. Card width is the single permitted variant — 212 full-bleed, 138 in home's railed layout — and nothing else varies: not the title's size, not its weight, not the pill's radius, not the avatar's. Three further details are decisions rather than styling. The count sits inside each arm of the pill, so the control is a single pressable thing in both states and its set state reads as a fill rather than a second colour, per DESIGN.md; the cost is one extra local article_stats region per card (the hidden arm's), both resolving in-tab against one collection. And name-or-handle needs no conditional: display_name is nullable and the markup has no if, so both are always rendered and .name:empty { display: none } with .name:not(:empty)::after { content: \" · \" } does the rest — which is also why setting a name changes every byline, comment and preview at once (accept-profile-propagates): each of them reads the same app_user row rather than a copy. And both arms of the pill are siblings of the Favorite probe, never children of it: a nested region inside a probe's item template binds against that region's row (screen.js:736-745), so a set arm nested inside the probe would read article_stats?article_id=eq.<favorite-uuid>, match nothing, fall through to data-empty-row and render ♥ 0 on exactly the pieces the reader has favorited — on seven screens, at the one moment accept-favorite-toggles and accept-favorite-live are checked. As siblings, {id} is the article row in both arms, the single declared read is correct as written, and the retraction is a filtered delete (decision 4). The pill fills optimistically on the press while the number follows a second later from the pipeline; the frames draw that order because it is the true one — home-favorited shows the fill flipped and the number unmoved, which is the seam, and the path's third beat is where the number lands. One last thing the storyboard has to hold to, because a fixture collision makes a frame prove nothing: one roster, one display name per handle. The signed-in reader is Ana Vega · velvet-crow-4 and appears as herself only in the shell chrome, in article-owner, article-delete-armed, profile-own-profile, editor-populated and settings-populated; everybody behind a Follow control is somebody else — Ivo Marek · amber-fox-7, Iris Okoye · jade-elk-83, Ines Fell · copper-wren-5 — and no handle ever carries two names"
	"decision-probe-empty": "A probe region's :empty state is the ownership and toggle primitive; both arms are always in the markup and CSS chooses. A region refresh sweeps the region down to exactly its current row nodes and removes the <template data-item> on the first pass (screen.js:803-807), and the <p class=\"empty\"> is appended only when data-empty is set (screen.js:821-826). So for a probe carrying no data-empty, region has rows ⇔ region:not(:empty) — a pure-CSS reading of an RLS answer, with no session placeholder, no conditional rendering (there is no data-if) and no second read. Every owner-only control and both toggles are built from it: .probe:empty ~ .when-set { display: none } and its inverse — a sibling combinator, which already tells you that the control cannot be inside the probe. Three rules the assembly must keep, and the first is now stated per probe shape, because one universal was wrong in one direction and impossible in the other. An ownership probe's control is a plain sibling: the comment × sits beside the comment row's me probe, wired against the enclosing comment (test-comment-remove states it correctly). A toggle probe's retraction is a sibling carrying data-filter: a bare sibling would call store.remove(\"favorite\", <articleId>) (wireForm, screen.js:778) and delete nothing at all, while a child would bind every nested region inside it — the count included — against the favorite row rather than the article (syncNested → hydrateRegion(el, entry.ctx, false), screen.js:736-745). The filtered form is the platform's own mechanism for exactly this: removeWhere resolves the matching ids with a GET under the reader's own token, so RLS scopes the delete to my own row without the markup ever naming me (data-crud.js:339-343, SPEC #Form.filter). So article_id=eq.{id} retracts a favorite and followed_id=eq.{id} (profile header) or followed_id=eq.{author_id} (article byline) retracts a follow, and both pill arms bind against the article row, which is what makes the one declared ArticleStats read correct in both. And every screen carrying a probe writes [data-state=\"loading\"] .arms { visibility: hidden }, because before the first refresh the <template> is still a child and the region reads :not(:empty) for one frame. Rejected: a click handler reading a session — handlers fire only on drag (screen.js:827-830) and are denied window, document and fetch by check-handlers.ts, so this is not a thing the platform can be asked for"
	"decision-public-read-owner-writes": "Public content with owner writes is public-read plus additive raw policies — never a declared owned widened in SQL. #Access has four modes and none of them is \"everyone reads, the owner writes\". public-read emits exactly a SELECT arm for app_user and the service catch-all (emit.cue:172-177), so a public-read table whose rows come from forms is unbuildable as declared: every insert is refused. 011_owner_writes.sql adds the missing arms — INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on article gated author_id = auth_uid(), INSERT/DELETE on comment, UPDATE on app_user gated id = auth_uid() — and drops nothing, because public-read put nothing there. The rejected alternative was declaring owned and widening SELECT in raw SQL. It produces correct server behaviour and a booby trap: emit.cue:305 mirrors the declared mode into shell.yaml, and visible() (data-crud.js:174-200) re-applies it in the browser on every collection read, knowing only the four declared modes — so the same table would mean \"mine\" on a locally-translated read and \"everyone's\" on a server-computed one, with a regex and a probe row deciding which. The rule this compile adopts: the declared mode is the mode the client must believe; widen only in the direction the mirror already allows, narrow freely. One column narrowing follows from the same rule, and it is load-bearing rather than tidy: an RLS policy gates which row may be updated and never which columns, and 002_grants.sql emits table-wide GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON ALL TABLES … TO app_user (emit.cue:612-618), so 011's USING (id = auth_uid()) would happily let a hand-made PATCH /crud/app_user?id=eq.<me> {\"handle\":\"…\"} through. 011 therefore also carries REVOKE UPDATE ON app_user FROM app_user; GRANT UPDATE (display_name, bio, image_url) ON app_user TO app_user; — narrowing, so safe under this entry's own rule, and PostgREST honours column privileges with 42501. Without it the brief's “it never changes” is untrue, and the whole of Me.handle's “cannot drift” justification — the thing that makes the denormalisation safe — rests on nothing. Two deliberate absences follow. comment gets no UPDATE arm — the brief puts editing a comment out of scope and the missing policy is what keeps that true even if a form ever appears — and article_tag gets no arm at all, because every row of it is the trigger's (decision 10). Backend charter: access models are this seat's, and both files carry the argument in their header comments"
	"decision-publish-checklist": "The editor asks for what it cannot refuse. Title and body are the only fields a publish is refused for; a standfirst, a cover and a tag are what separate a piece that gets read from one that scrolls past, and the reference apps steer writers toward all three rather than accepting their absence silently. So the bar states them as invitations with the reason attached, and each line reads met or unmet from its own control's :placeholder-shown — no state to keep, nothing to reset, and no second definition of \"empty\" to drift from the form's own. It is deliberately not a validation summary: nothing it lists blocks publishing, and a writer who wants none of it is not stopped"
	"decision-publish-lands-one-tap": "Publishing lands the writer on the finished piece in one tap, not zero. wireForm ends a successful create with form.reset(), success for 600 ms and a return to base (screen.js:515,604-619): there is no location change, forms cannot navigate and write at once, and handlers fire only on drag. So nothing in the platform lands a writer on the row they just created, and accept-publish-lands is realized as a live region beneath the form reading the writer's own pieces newest-first (article?author_id=eq.{id}&order=created_at.desc), the first of which is rendered as a link and the rest hidden by .latest > *:not(:first-child) { display: none }. The absence of limit=1 is the whole mechanism, not an oversight: limit is outside parseFilter's translatable subset (data-crud.js:49-72), so a region carrying it takes the PostgREST path — and a server-fetched row cannot see the optimistic overlay and carries no $synced key, so the line would flip on settlement rather than on the press and could never wear the terminal's unconfirmed-write badge. author_id=eq.{id} alone resolves in-tab against the synced collection, which is where the optimistic row lives. So the sequence the success frame draws is the true one: the title flips on the press; the row wears data-pending=\"true\" and the frame draws “· sending” in place of the link; and [data-pending=\"true\"] .landing { display: none } holds the anchor back until the txid confirms — necessarily, because slug is GENERATED ALWAYS … STORED with no form field, so the optimistic row has no slug and #/article/{slug} would resolve to #/article/, which matchRoute rejects and currentRoute() throws on (shell.js:226-235) — an uncaught exception, not a 404. Rejected: a navigate form after the create (two submits, and the second would need the slug the first generated), and a redirect handler (there is no submit handler). This is a gesture-cost deviation from the brief's plain reading, so it is stated here for the ux and qa seats to sign explicitly rather than discover in verify"
	"decision-reading-progress": "How far into a piece the reader is, is drawn by the scroller — not by code. A progress hairline is the one reading affordance every long-form surface has settled on, and the obvious way to build it is a scroll listener, which this app cannot have: handlers fire on drag alone and decision 25 keeps the compartment count at zero. The affordance is available anyway, because CSS grew the capability: animation-timeline: scroll(root block) binds a two-frame keyframe to the document's own scroll progress, so the bar is a stylesheet rule with nothing to schedule and nothing to run on a frame that does not scroll. Two details are load-bearing rather than tidy. It is wrapped in @supports (animation-timeline: scroll()), because a browser without scroll-driven animations would run the same declaration as an ordinary animation of zero duration with both fill and paint the bar full on arrival — the one wrong answer, and worse than absence; unsupported, the rule outside the block leaves it display: none. The same wrong answer arrives by a second road and is closed the same way: a piece too short to scroll leaves the timeline inactive, which applies no effect at all, so the rule declares transform: scaleX(0) outright and lets the animation override it while it runs — measured, not assumed (a gone frame painted a full accent line before it was). And the storybook hides it: a frame is a box on a page of boxes and a viewport-fixed element would draw one accent line across the top of the review page per frame, none of them about the frame beneath it. The accent is spent here — the only accent in this screen's chrome — because progress through a piece is the reading surface talking about itself, not navigation, which DESIGN.md holds to secondary"
	"decision-reading-time": "The reading cost is a stored generated column on the article, not a pipeline and not a number a writer types. It is a pure function of the body — words over 220 a minute, rounded up, floored at one — so the two alternatives both buy a way for the piece and its cost to disagree. A derived table would add a CDC round trip and a window in which a just-edited article advertises its old length; a typed field would let them disagree permanently, and would ask a writer for a number they are worse at estimating than arithmetic is. A generated column cannot drift, because there is no moment at which the body exists and the count does not. 220 words a minute is the median adult prose rate, and the figure Medium and Substack both round to — a reader who has calibrated against one of them has calibrated against this. The expression must be IMMUTABLE, which is what a generated column requires and what rules out anything that renders the markdown first: it splits on whitespace (regexp_split_to_array over btrim), so syntax counts as words. On heavily formatted prose that over-counts by a few per cent, which is accepted — the number is a reader's expectation-setter, not a measurement, and the alternative is a markdown parser in SQL"
	"decision-show-and-refuse": "The right control is offered, and the server refuses the wrong write anyway — both, in that order. accept-author-only-controls demands a visible contrast (\"only an article's author is offered edit and delete; another person's article offers follow and favorite instead\"), so visibility rides the Me probe of decision 4. But visibility is not enforcement: a hand-typed /editor/<someone-else's-id> renders, and its save matches no policy in 011, so PostgREST answers 4xx, data-crud.js raises a NonRetriableError, and refused (screen.js:589-592) unhides .store-error and sets validation-error — the reader is told \"This piece isn't yours to change.\" in --danger, with nothing saved. That refusal is storyboarded as edit-article-validation-error, deliberately, because it is this app's headline behaviour and the brief asks for it in those words: \"Conduit refuses the rest rather than hiding the button\". Rejected: hiding the route (nothing can hide a hash), and trusting the probe alone (a CSS rule is not a security boundary and this ir will not pretend otherwise)"
	"decision-sink-keys": "A sink read as a list keys on id; a sink read only as a nested singleton keys on its subject. A list region keys its item nodes on String(row.id) (screen.js:756), so a sink without an id column collapses every row onto one node — the kind of defect that renders as \"one tag in the popular panel\" and looks like a data problem. TagCount and FavoriteIndex are read as lists and therefore key on id: for TagCount the tag word is the identity (and /tag/{id} is the link), for FavoriteIndex it is the composite <user_id>:<article_id>. ArticleStats is only ever a nested singleton, so it keeps article_id as its pk and rides shell.yaml keys, which is derived automatically because a screen reads the entity (emit.cue:304,346). None of the three carries a seed: a per-subject sink's row appears with its first event, and the reading region carries data-empty-row='{\"favorite_count\":0}' so an article nobody has favorited renders 0 rather than nothing"
	"decision-sink-no-fk": "No sink carries a foreign key to article. Deleting an article cascades its favorites and tag rows away; each cascaded DELETE is a CDC event whose back-filled before-image carries the dead article_id, and decision 11's key union is what makes the pipeline emit a final {article_id, 0} so the count does not freeze. If the sink column were a foreign key, that final POST would violate it, retry three times and wedge the stream — on every article deletion, which is a routine act. The cost of the alternative is an inert orphan row that nothing reads and no screen can reach. Wedging a pipeline is a class of failure the app cannot recover from; a stale row nobody looks at is not. FavoriteIndex.user_id keeps its FK, because no flow deletes a person and it is what lets the profile read filter by handle without denormalising one. What this entry bought is recorded here rather than discovered in profiling: with no foreign key from favorite_index.article_id to article there is no embedded article resource to join, so profile-favorites cannot state its list in one read and nests a live Article region per row instead — a server-computed GET per card, bounded by that list's own limit=20, re-fired on article and on app_user (decision 6). A wedged pipeline on every article deletion is still much the worse failure, so the trade stands — but a reader of this entry should be able to see that it has a price and where it is paid"
	"decision-slug": "The readable address is the title plus the first hex group of the row's own uuid. accept-slug-readable asks for an address derived from the title that still tells two same-titled articles apart. A generated column takes IMMUTABLE expressions over the row's own columns only — no subqueries, no other rows, no now(), and notably no loop for a free numeric suffix — so the row's own identity is the only discriminator available: coalesce(nullif(btrim(left(regexp_replace(lower(title), '[^a-z0-9]+', '-', 'g'), 80), '-'), ''), 'article') || '-' || left(id::text, 8), STORED UNIQUE. Measured in Postgres: how-to-train-your-dragon-4f5df9a9 and how-to-train-your-dragon-7f2af670 for two articles titled How to Train Your Dragon!, and article-469615a1 for a title of pure punctuation. UNIQUE is kept: it costs an index the /article/:slug read wants anyway and it is the backstop for the ~1-in-10⁸ collision, which reaches the writer as a readable .store-error rather than a corrupted route. Two consequences owned rather than discovered: the column is GENERATED ALWAYS, so retitling changes the address and an old link 404s (which is why the edit route is keyed on the id — decision 22); and the address carries eight characters no human chose, the price of \"readable and unique\" without a uniquifying loop. Rejected: a client-minted slug field (two writers, one word, and a race), and a trigger that loops for a free suffix (a third trigger, and a write that can fail on contention)"
	"decision-terminal-chrome": "The app draws no sign-out button, no back button, no toast, no modal, and no upload control. The terminal builds the nav from the non-parametrized routes in declaration order and appends <display name> · <handle> · sign out at its right edge, with no opt-out on the sign-out itself (shell.js:198-241,366-390); /settings therefore names where sign-out lives (\"Sign out is in the bar above, beside your handle.\") and draws nothing. A second sign-out button would be an unreviewed copy of a platform affordance that behaves differently from every other app for no reason a user benefits from — the prelude's capability doctrine, stated as a rule this compile obeyed rather than a principle it admired. The same reasoning covers the refusal vocabulary: there is no modal, dialog, toast or confirm, so a refusal is the form's .invalid and .store-error paragraphs, one treatment everywhere, storyboarded twice and reused verbatim. And capabilities.blobs stays off: the brief scopes a profile picture as \"a link the person supplies\", so there is no file control, and the cluster drops rclone-s3 and imgproxy with it. Declining a capability the app plausibly wants is a Decisions entry by the prelude's own rule — that is what this entry is, for blobs, and it is also what it is for motion. #Design.motion exists so an app can retune the terminal's row-arrival, screen-change and refusal-arrival timings, and this app declares it empty: the terminal's published defaults are accepted as they are, because a reading app's motion vocabulary is rows arriving and screens changing, which is exactly what the terminal already times at moments no screen can observe. The consequence is worth stating rather than assuming: DESIGN.md's “don't animate longer than 300ms” is therefore a property of the terminal, verified once against its defaults, and not a rule this app restates in its own stylesheets — where it would have nothing to govern, since a screen that animates has taken over a transition the terminal drives"
	"decision-updated-at-hidden-now": "updated_at is a hidden {now} on the revise form, not a trigger. A column default fires only on INSERT, so \"when it was last changed\" needs a writer on UPDATE. resolveHidden (screen.js:222) resolves a hidden field's {now} to a submit-time ISO string, which is exactly one line of form contract. A third trigger next to the two this design already spends would be machinery for one column, and each trigger is raw SQL a reviewer must read. Accepted loss: the timestamp is the browser's clock at submit rather than the database's at commit, which can differ by the round-trip and by whatever the device's clock believes. Nothing in Conduit orders or filters by updated_at — every list is created_at.desc — so the drift is display-only, and the field is not user-facing on any frame"
}

